# Email and digests

A notification can go out by email as well as to the inbox, one at a time
or gathered into a digest. Email and push are
[background jobs](/docs/jobs/overview), queued in the same transaction
that saves the notification and sent by your app's jobs runner, which
retries what fails, so a slow or broken mail provider never holds up the
request that sent the notification.

## Email

Give the notifier a [mailer](/docs/mail/overview), a way to find each
user's address, and a function that writes the email for a notification:

```gleam
let notifier =
  notifications.new(db)
  |> notifications.with_base_url("https://example.com")
  |> notifications.with_email(
    mailer,
    address: fn(user_id) { users.email_address(db, user_id) },
    render: fn(n) {
      mail.message()
      |> mail.subject(n.title)
      |> mail.text(n.body <> "\n\n" <> "https://example.com" <> option.unwrap(n.link, "/"))
    },
  )
```

`render` gives the subject and body; the recipient is filled in. An address
that cannot be found gives up on that email. Kinds whose delivery says
`EmailNow` are then emailed one by one.

### In the recipient's language

The title and body are whatever you wrote when sending, so write them in
the recipient's language, or keep the parts in `data` and write the email
in `render`. With [Howdy Auth](/docs/auth/overview), each user has a
`locale`, the language they registered in or chose, which also works for
emails sent outside a request:

```gleam
fn render(n: Notification) -> mail.Message {
  let t = case users.get(identity, n.recipient) {
    Ok(User(locale: Some(tag), ..)) -> i18n.new(tag)
    _ -> i18n.new("en")
  }
  mail.message()
  |> mail.subject(messages.notification_subject(t, title: n.title))
  |> mail.text(n.body)
}
```

See [internationalisation](/docs/i18n/overview) for the catalogues behind
`messages`.

### Only if not read first

Someone already looking at the app does not need an email as well.
`email_after` waits before sending, and skips the email if the
notification was read in the meantime:

```gleam
notifications.email_after(notifier, 120)
```

## Digests

Kinds whose delivery says `EmailDigest` are gathered into one email per
user, at most once per period:

```gleam
notifications.with_digest(notifier, every: 86_400, render: fn(unread) {
  mail.message()
  |> mail.subject(int.to_string(list.length(unread)) <> " new notifications")
  |> mail.template(digest_email(unread))
})
```

`render` is given the user's unread digest notifications, oldest first, up
to a hundred. Ones read in the inbox before the digest goes are left out,
and a user who has read everything gets no email. Without `with_digest`,
digest kinds are emailed one at a time.

## Sending them

Teach your app's [jobs runner](/docs/jobs/overview#Doing-jobs) to deliver
notifications, and run it under your supervision tree, before the server:

```gleam
let runner =
  jobs.runner(db)
  |> jobs.queue("notifications", concurrency: 10)
  |> notifications.handle_jobs(notifier)

supervisor.new(supervisor.OneForOne)
|> supervisor.add(jobs.supervised(runner))
|> supervisor.add(howdy.supervised(app(notifier)))
|> howdy.start_application(name: "my_app_server")
```

The jobs go to the `notifications` queue, ten at a time on each node
unless the runner gives it another concurrency, and show in the
[admin's Jobs page](/docs/jobs/production#Watching-jobs) with the rest.
Sending is quick: the runner hears about the jobs as soon as the
notification is saved. Run the runner on every node and they share the
work. As with every job, a node that dies mid-send leaves the job for
another to run again, so a crash can mean a second copy but never a lost
one.

- An email waits `email_after` seconds, then is sent, unless the
  notification was read in the meantime: its job is cancelled instead.
- A digest is one job per user, due when their last digest is a period
  old. Notifications sent while it waits join it; one sent while it is
  going out waits for the next.
- Each [push service](/docs/notifications/web-push#Other-push-services)
  is a job of its own, so a service that is down holds up only itself.
- What fails is tried again, with the delay doubling from thirty seconds,
  five times in all; then it is given up and logged. A mail provider that
  is down or rate limiting is retried; one that refuses the message, or a
  user with no address, is not.

## Testing

`howdy/mail/outbox` keeps mail instead of sending it, so a test can read
what went out:

```gleam
let box = outbox.start()
let notifier = notifications.new(db) |> notifications.with_email(mail.mailer(outbox.adapter(box)), ...)
notifications.draft("security.login", "New sign-in") |> notifications.send(notifier, to: user_id)
let runner = jobs.runner(db) |> notifications.handle_jobs(notifier)
let assert Ok(_) = jobs.drain(runner)
let assert Ok(email) = outbox.latest_to(box, "ana@example.com")
```
